<interrogationes unitas="sigillum">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compute the SHA-256 of a buffer and print it as hex?
    <responsum symbolum="sigillum_computare sigillum_hex">Sigillum
      s = sigillum_computare(datum, mensura); then sigillum_hex(&amp;s,
      hex) with a buffer of at least SIGILLUM_HEX_MENSURA (65) bytes:
      it writes 64 lowercase hex digits plus a NUL. For a chorda pass
      c.datum and c.mensura; no NUL is needed and none is read. The
      raw 32 bytes are in s.octeti.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I hash data that arrives in pieces?
    <responsum symbolum="sigillum_incipere sigillum_addere sigillum_finire">Declare
      a SigillumContextus on the stack, call sigillum_incipere, then
      sigillum_addere for each piece (any sizes; the result equals the
      one-shot hash of the concatenation) and sigillum_finire once.
      finire pads the context in place and leaves massa_mensura at 64,
      so the context is spent: a further sigillum_addere or a second
      sigillum_finire on it writes past the 64-byte partial block (out
      of bounds, not just a wrong digest). Call sigillum_incipere again
      before reusing it. Nothing is allocated.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I hash a shared prefix once and then finish several different digests from it?
    <responsum symbolum="sigillum_addere sigillum_finire SigillumContextus">Yes:
      SigillumContextus is a plain struct with no pointers (state,
      partial block, length), so copying it with = forks the hash.
      Feed the prefix, copy the context for each branch, add the
      branch data and finish each copy.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compare two digests, for example to check a token or MAC?
    <responsum symbolum="sigillum_aequale">sigillum_aequale(&amp;a,
      &amp;b). It runs in constant time (it ORs the XOR of all 32
      bytes instead of stopping at the first difference like memcmp),
      so it is safe where the digest guards a secret. Either pointer
      NIHIL gives FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I compute an HMAC-SHA256, for example to sign an AWS SigV4 request?
    <responsum symbolum="sigillum_hmac">sigillum_hmac(clavis,
      mensura_clavis, datum, mensura_datorum) implements RFC 2104: a
      key longer than 64 bytes is hashed first, a shorter one is
      zero-padded, and an empty key is allowed. Verified against RFC
      4231 vectors. It is one-shot; there is no incremental HMAC
      context.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I parse a 64-character hex string back into a Sigillum?
    <responsum symbolum="sigillum_hex" via="lib/thesaurus.c">Not
      with this library: it only goes from digest to hex. lib/thesaurus.c
      has its own private _ex_hex (64 LOWERCASE hex digits, refusing
      anything else); copy that pattern or ask for a public
      sigillum_ex_hex rather than writing another private
      one.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is there both sigillum and friatio, two hash libraries?
    <responsum symbolum="sigillum_computare" via="include/friatio.h lib/sigillum.worklog.md">friatio
      hashes to DISPERSE keys over hash-table buckets, where speed
      matters and a collision only costs a probe. sigillum hashes to
      IDENTIFY content (content-addressed stores, ledger entities,
      versions of reducers), where a collision would merge two
      different things, so collision resistance is load-bearing and
      SHA-256 is used.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
